Z||000000||MontanaFiero||04-26-2001||02:56 PM||||Hey everyone, I just learned some things that I thought I'd share with you about converting a/c from Freon (R-12) to the newer and cheaper (R-134A). Freon has gone up to $100 a pound now so it is far more economical to convert your system to 134A when it needs to be charged again.<P>You will see conversion kits in stores like Wal-Mart, K-mart, various auto stores, etc for about $39. But beware-- Those kits are not all you will be needing to do this conversion properly. You can't just "pour in" the new 134A  refrigerant because the R-12 is not compatible and will eventually freeze your compressor. Big $$$$.<P>1) Have the system professionally "evacuated" (all old freon removed) and flushed.<P>the rest you should be able to do yourself.<P>2) buy and O-ring kit along with the conversion kit, and a gauge. Make sure you get ester oil (134A compatible oil).<P>3)Replace the accumulator (receiver/dryer) -- this costs about $75, but it must be replaced. Also replace the expansion valve.(only about $5)<P>4)Change all of the o-rings<P>5)Follow the instructions in the haynes manual (not chilton) and on the conversion kit to charge the system. Make sure it is not overcharged, but keep in mind our system takes about as much freon as a minivan because of how far it has to go.<P>Feel free to comment on this.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-26-2001||07:19 PM||||You might want to do allot of reading at <A HREF="http://www.aircondition.com" TARGET=_blank>www.aircondition.com</A>  before a conversion.<P>Make sure that when you install the federally required conversion label that you put the oil type on it. This will save you headaches later.<P>||||reg||1
